  "design": ["§6 Forget contract: ids plus expected revisions; tombstones; suppressed source ids; derived `source_forgotten`; the hard guarantee across index rebuild, restart and pending-write recovery; the retention boundary named in the result", "§6 Derived supersession: a tombstone keeps its `supersedes` list and origin, so forgetting a replacement does not revive its predecessor", "§7 Capabilities: memory_forget prompts on an attended turn and is refused unattended", "§7a memory_forget result, control snapshot tombstones"],
  "loads_in": ["slice-2", "slice-3"],
  "notes_for_implementer": "F (favourite colour) was extracted from message 4812; C (a birthday reminder) cites the same message; R (a replacement) supersedes O. Step 1 is the unattended refusal — nothing changes. Step 2 forgets F from an attended model-tool turn with the card approved: the file and its `.history` go, the tombstone carries `message:4812`, and the result names the transcript copy that remains. Steps 3–5: C is derived `source_forgotten` — out of automatic recall, still readable and searchable with that flag. Steps 6–8: erase the index and restart; nothing about F comes back. Step 9: re-running extraction over message 4812 writes nothing (suppressed source). Steps 10–13: forget R; O stays superseded because the tombstone's `supersedes` and origin still qualify. Step 14: editing C through a user surface lifts `source_forgotten`. Slice 2 runs everything but steps 1, 9 and 14 through the library; the `source_forgotten` flag has no §7a wire field, so the loader reads it from the search result's `sources_status` (a pinned name the report lists as a design gap).",
